{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018u1499DRPrQnjSPCeRpXkq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\begin{abstract}\n2\tRecent methods for collision avoidance in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models report high Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) on safety benchmarks and claim effective obstacle avoidance. We show that these metrics can be systematically contaminated by action collapse: the model ceases to produce meaningful motion, and the resulting inaction is counted as successful avoidance. On the SafeLIBERO benchmark, a Signed Distance Field (SDF)-augmented VLA reports 91.5\\% CAR alongside 0\\% task success, yet 99.6\\% of its safe episodes exhibit end-effector displacement below 0.1\\,mm. This contamination is not method-specific; a baseline without any collision objective achieves 82\\% CAR with 99.4\\% of safe episodes below the same threshold, and Pi0.5, which achieves 74.7\\% task success, shows the same pattern. We propose a corrective metric, displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\\text{f}$), that excludes sub-threshold episodes. Two structural issues compound the problem. First, Task Success Rate (TSR) and CAR are anti-correlated: models that move the robot through the workspace collide, while collapsed models appear safe. Second, ground-truth SDF oracle experiments demonstrate that end-effector-only refinement cannot prevent full-body collisions, as SDF gradients that repel the end-effector simultaneously drive arm links into obstacle volumes. Preliminary results indicate that training-time SDF supervision on a sufficiently capable VLA can produce genuine collision avoidance with zero frozen rate. Our findings establish that displacement filtering is necessary for meaningful VLA safety evaluation.\n3\t\\end{abstract}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013nCxBRTTrC7C4kDzoJmBr2","session_id":"2e6dd661-5539-4780-b46e-9be6f0e5cc6e","uuid":"867b66e8-f7d5-4906-b8c9-4ed66680245f","timestamp":"2026-05-26T03:28:24.516Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01R5qRqHJjnYjThKuyfDwj4w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019sZhdD2rztV1woZFbTXoXh","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/diff_collision_sdf/docs/paper/experimental_framework.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1278,"cache_read_input_tokens":38103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1278,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013nCxBRTTrC7C4kDzoJmBr2","session_id":"2e6dd661-5539-4780-b46e-9be6f0e5cc6e","uuid":"d31b1212-3dcd-42df-aec3-53155740078a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TNGpSNZYiA9p5qSBcjieBS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LUrkt7oz4HawEx7an8uZYu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Venue Context\nCoRL (Conference on Robot Learning). Recent acceptance rate ~39%. CoRL 2024 used a 1–4 overall score scale. Review dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning.\n\n## Your Review Scope\nFocus on: (1) novelty of contributions, (2) positioning against related work, (3) whether the problem is important and well-motivated, (4) writing quality, (5) broader impact and relevance to robot learning community, (6) missing related work or unfair comparisons.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\"\n\n8-page paper (5 references). The paper identifies \"fake safety\" in VLA evaluation — where standard CAR (collision avoidance rate) is contaminated by action collapse (frozen robots scored as collision-free). \n\n**Four claimed contributions:**\n1. Identifies and quantifies fake safety in VLA evaluation — standard CAR is systematically contaminated by action collapse\n2. Proposes dfCAR (displacement-filtered CAR) — corrected metric invariant to threshold in δ∈[2mm,200mm]\n3. Characterizes EE-only SDF structural limitation — SDF pushes EE away but arm body sweeps through obstacles\n4. Demonstrates LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demos produces genuine collision avoidance WITHOUT geometric supervision; SDF is largely redundant at standard λ, marginal at extreme λ\n\n**Key results:**\n- Pi0 full FT: 91.5% CAR but 0% TSR, 99.6% frozen episodes → \"fake safety\"\n- dfCAR threshold invariance due to 6-order-of-magnitude EE displacement bimodality\n- LoRA-only: 52.8% CAR (3 seeds), +26pp over Pi0.5 baseline, 0% fake safety\n- SDF λ=0.005/0.01: no benefit over LoRA-only (p=0.24/0.48)\n- SDF λ=0.1: marginal +5.7pp (p=0.15), not significant\n- Phase 2 (SDF 10K→λ=0 20K): weak persistence +6.7pp (p=0.12), TSR=16.5%\n- ALL conditions: catastrophic TSR collapse 73%→4-7%\n\n**Related Work section (Section 2) covers:**\n- VLA models (Pi0, Pi0.5, OpenVLA) — brief\n- Collision avoidance in robotics (classical SDF, integration into learned policies) — brief\n- Safety evaluation metrics (CAR, SafeLIBERO) — brief\n\nThe paper has only 5 references total: Pi0, OpenVLA, SafeLIBERO, LoRA, CHOMP.\n\n**Section structure:**\n1. Introduction (1.5 pages)\n2. Related Work (0.5 page)\n3. Method (1 page): Problem setting, dfCAR definition, SDF auxiliary loss, LoRA fine-tuning\n4. Experiments (3 pages): Fake safety, dfCAR threshold, capability-safety anti-correlation, SDF structural limitation, fine-tuning attribution, lambda dose-response, Phase 2 persistence\n5. Discussion (1.5 pages)\n6. Conclusion (0.5 page)\n\n**Limitations acknowledged by authors:**\n(i) Only SafeLIBERO with Pi0/Pi0.5 — generalizability unknown\n(ii) n=3 seeds, limited statistical power\n(iii) spatial_bowl missing LoRA-only control\n(iv) Only EE-only SDF evaluated, not full-body SDF\n(v) Phase 2 based on 2 seeds — preliminary\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\nFrom the experiment design (review_context.yaml), the paper covers most planned experiments EXCEPT:\n- spatial_bowl LoRA-only control (NOT COMPLETED — GPU resources exhausted) → cannot do cross-scene SDF attribution\n\nThe paper's narrative was adjusted from \"clean negative\" to \"predominantly negative with boundary conditions\" due to: LoRA seed2 pulling down the 3-seed mean, making the \"SDF = LoRA\" claim weaker (p=0.24 instead of clearly indistinguishable).\n\n## Instructions\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty/positioning/writing/impact)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty/related work/positioning/missing comparisons)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores** (1-4 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Accept, 4=Strong Accept) for: Overall, Novelty, Significance, Clarity, Confidence (1-4: 1=low, 4=expert)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: Is this paper a meaningful contribution to the robot learning community? Is the \"fake safety\" finding genuinely novel or widely known? Is dfCAR a real contribution or trivially obvious? Are the SDF redundancy claims sufficiently supported? Is the related work adequate?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":708,"cache_read_input_tokens":71342,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":708},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e6dd661-5539-4780-b46e-9be6f0e5cc6e","uuid":"7b23c611-3eb0-48b0-a74c-f1eab5a3ac9a"}
